<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert II of Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1397–1439</span> · Also known as Albert V of Austria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> from 1404 and, as son-in-law of Emperor Sigismund, became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1437–1438 and king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1438. He died in 1439 returning from a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and his posthumous son Ladislaus V was drawn into a long succession struggle in Hungary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-ii-of-germany.jpg" alt="Albert II of Germany" width="264" height="526" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albrecht_II_von_Deutschland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
